<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwasi Boachi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kwasi Boakye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwasi Boachi, or Kwasi Boakye, was a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwaku-dua-i">Kwaku Dua I</a>, sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> with his cousin Kwame Poku in 1837 as part of negotiations over the recruitment of Ashanti soldiers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> Army. Unlike his cousin, who returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, graduating as a mining engineer from the Royal Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in 1847 and attending lectures at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg-mining-academy">Freiberg Mining Academy</a>. Sent to the Dutch East Indies in 1850, he suffered discrimination from his superior and in 1857 received compensation, including an estate south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Buitenzorg</a>, where he died in 1904.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwasi-boachi.jpg" alt="Kwasi Boachi" width="330" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Possibly made by C. Vogel von Vogelstein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwasi_Boakye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Asante studied in Europe: in 1837 <b>Kwasi Boachi</b>, a son of Kwaku Dua I, and his cousin Kwame Poku were sent to the Netherlands for a European education.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li></ul></section>
